                  "candidateEvaluations": [
                    {
                      "id": "candidate-02",
                      "version": "agent.2",
                      "label": "Fold the constant bias into kernel 1 and drop the separate subtract: 6 -> 3 elementwise kernels",
                      "title": "Fold the constant bias into kernel 1 and drop the separate subtract: 6 -> 3 elementwise kernels",
                      "hypothesis": "The measured 15.36us baseline and the 10.24us of the 4-kernel candidate-01 imply ~2.5us p50 per kernel launch at this size. Folding -0.125 into the first kernel via a cached 0-dim bias tensor (torch.add(bias, x, alpha=1.25)) removes the standalone sub kernel, and keeping the add-with-alpha + in-place relu chain yields exactly 3 launches, so p50 should land near 7-8us, a further ~25% below candidate-01 and ~50% below baseline.",
                      "change": "run(inputs) now computes out = torch.add(bias, x, alpha=1.25) (kernel 1, out-of-place, x and y never mutated), then out.add_(y, alpha=0.75) (kernel 2), then out.relu_() (kernel 3) and returns the freshly allocated buffer directly, with no clone and no scratch tensors. A module-level _BIAS dict caches one 0-dim float32 tensor(-0.125) per (device, dtype) so no host->device transfer happens per call. Arithmetic order differs from reference only by association ((1.25x - 0.125) + 0.75y vs (1.25x + 0.75y) - 0.125), which is ~1 ulp at these magnitudes and far inside the atol/rtol 1e-5 contract. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are untouched.",
                      "files": "run.py",
